data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1793797754535,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"Crew was Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin, all on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Saturn V launched from Kennedy Space Center in Florida on July 16 at 13:32 UTC.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The command module housed the three astronauts and was the only part to return to Earth; the service module provided propulsion, power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard CM Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Command Module\",\"Service Module\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\",\"Columbia\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The two spent about two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, speaking with President Nixon, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"The crew rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 fulfilled a goal President John F. Kennedy set in May 1961 to land a man on the Moon before the decade ended.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"The program recovered from the fatal Apollo 1 launchpad fire in January 1967 and built on Mercury and Gemini technologies.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Space Race\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union could not match the Saturn V rocket, and its uncrewed Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21 while Armstrong and Aldrin were on the surface.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, roughly one-fifth of the world's population.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Stepping onto the lunar surface, Armstrong declared: \\\"That's one small step for [a] man, one giant leap for mankind.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Returned lunar samples led to identifying three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments kept returning data for years.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom. Columbia is preserved at the National Air and Space Museum.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Columbia\",\"Eagle\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"United States\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, surprising the world and fueling fears about Soviet capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"On April 12, 1961, Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit Earth; Alan Shepard followed on May 5, 1961 with a suborbital flight.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Space Race\",\"Sputnik crisis\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a lunar challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the decade is out.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said no single space project would be more impressive to mankind or more important for long-range space exploration, and none so difficult or expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing larger liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding engine development and unmanned exploration.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy argued that going to the Moon would be a national effort, since all Americans must work to put one man there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University's football stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said outer space had no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its hazards were hostile to all mankind.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said space conquest deserved the best of all mankind and its chance for peaceful cooperation might never come again.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy compared the Moon goal to climbing the highest mountain, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas, concluding 'We choose to go to the Moon.'\"}],\"entities\":[\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Moon\",\"United States\",\"mankind\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy argued the US should go to the Moon this decade because the hard challenge would organize and measure the best energies and skills.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition, and Norbert Wiener called it a \\\"moondoggle.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"The effort to land a man on the Moon was named Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"In June 1961, Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing to Khrushchev, who declined; Kennedy repeated the joint proposal at the UN in 1963.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"After Kennedy's death, the idea of a joint Moon mission was abandoned; an early key decision chose lunar orbit rendezvous over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"direct ascent\",\"Earth orbit rendezvous\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"A space rendezvous is an orbital maneuver in which two spacecraft navigate through space and meet up.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced that lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for Apollo, which would have three major parts.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"The command module held a cabin for three astronauts and was the only part that returned to Earth; the service module supplied propulsion, power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"The lunar module had a descent stage for landing on the Moon and an ascent stage to return astronauts to lunar orbit, allowing launch by a single Saturn V.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"Technologies for Apollo were developed by Project Gemini, and NASA adopted MOSFETs in the Interplanetary Monitoring Platform and silicon IC chips in the Apollo Guidance Computer.\"}],\"entities\":[\"James Webb\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo spacecraft\",\"Command module\",\"Service module\",\"Lunar module\",\"Saturn V\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee, and abruptly halted Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"In October 1968, Apollo 7 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"In December 1968, Apollo 8 tested the command module in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"In March 1969, Apollo 9 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit; in May, Apollo 10 conducted a 'dress rehearsal' in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"By July 1969, all was in readiness for Apollo 11 to take the final step onto the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Command Module\",\"Service Module\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\",\"Columbia\",\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Space Race\",\"Luna 15\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"Sputnik crisis\",\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"mankind\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"direct ascent\",\"Earth orbit rendezvous\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo spacecraft\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"1285d37a6f0af9a08f16bb64e7b97891b1a390cc21d72d911483ee0ef40d140f"}}

